//? Title: Integer Newton division: approximate, then correct exactly
//? Subtitle: Actual double reciprocal seeds, full-width guards and exact quotient comparisons
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Use a floating reciprocal estimate to propose an unsigned64-bit quotient, then
certify and correct it with exact wide products. The original four divisions and
half-seed reciprocal experiment are retained through canonical APIs. Actual
operative histories distinguish the existing five-update hardware-divide seed
from the separate educational convergence example, while full-width controls
cover the old conversion and increment-wrap hazards.
)

\toc

> Exactness comes from the final inequalities

For nonzero D, quotient q and remainder r are uniquely specified by qD≤N<(q+1)D
and r=N−qD. A reciprocal approximation can propose q, but those exact integer
inequalities certify it. The canonical producer first corrects downward while
qD>N, then upward while(q+1)D≤N. Native unsigned128 products hold the
complete64-by64 values; q is promoted before adding1, including q=2⁶⁴−1.

The existing implementation is a double-reciprocal method, not the fixed-point
algorithm claimed by the old prose. For N≥D>0 it computes d=double(D),
initializes x=1/d with an actual floating divide and executes exactly five
double updates x←x(2−d*x). The estimate double(N)*x is then converted to an
integer candidate. Five updates from an already accurate division seed need not
visibly improve it. No division-free whole-call, O(log operand-width)
multiprecision complexity or hardware speed claim follows from this fixed64-bit
routine.

> A small exact correction certificate

For100/7, the final candidate14 passes14·7≤100, while15·7>100 stops upward
correction. The diagram reads both actual final false comparison records from
the producer. Successful correction records, when present, precede these
terminating checks. The table prints exact products through the native wide
formatter, never by converting them to floating values.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/integer_newton_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/division.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::integer_newton_division_trace(100, 7);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| actual check index | direction | quotient before | exact tested product | changed " "candidate | quotient after |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | --- | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.checks.size(); ++i) {
        auto &c = t.checks[i];
        auto product = dm::algorithms::division_wide_decimal(c.tested_product);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | `{}` | {} | {} |\n", i,
                   c.downward ? "down" : "up", c.quotient_before, product,
                   c.changed, c.quotient_after);
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {8. * i, 0},
               std::format("Actual {} check\nproduct {} / changed {}",
                           c.downward ? "down" : "up", product, c.changed));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i));
    }
    std::print("\nActual certified quotient {}, remainder {}.\n",
               t.analysis.result.quotient, t.analysis.result.remainder);
    d.save_svg("build/integer_newton_tiny.svg");
}
```
